{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>You stretched out Your right hand, the earth swallowed them.</b> The sense is: after You blew with Your wind and the sea covered them, <b>You stretched out</b> against them <b>Your right hand</b> and Your arm, <b>and the earth swallowed them.</b> The meaning is that after they drowned, the sea cast them up as is the way of the sea, as it says (Exodus 14:30), \"Israel saw the Egyptians dead on the seashore\" — and there they would have been consumed and the dust would have settled upon the earth as it was. But instead they were swallowed and destroyed, as in \"together on every side You swallowed me\" (Job 10:8), \"HaShem swallowed up without pity\" (Lamentations 2:2), and \"those who are your ways have swallowed\" (Isaiah 3:12) — meaning destroyed. Our Sages said (Mekhilta here) that the earth opened its mouth and swallowed them, for they merited burial as a reward for having said, \"HaShem is the righteous one\" (Exodus 9:27). For the expression \"right hand\" and \"outstretched hand\" denotes only vengeance and destruction. Or perhaps they interpret <b>You stretched out Your right hand</b> as killing them in the sea, and <b>the earth swallowed them</b> afterward — and that is the burial which they merited:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Isaiah 3:12 in shortened form; the phrase דרך אורחותיך בלעו is rendered hyper-literally as 'those who are your ways have swallowed,' following his citation rather than the full verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:08.979954384Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}